		<section name="node">
			<attribute key="id" type="int">43</attribute>
			<attribute key="label" type="String">Following are some hints to put the logic in words.

1. As I process 1000 values, it will be difficult for me to remember the frequency number in my memory. So, I will take a paper and write down the number with its frequency. That way, for every value, I will update the paper and I don't need to worry about forgetting.
2. If it is a small list like 3 or 5 values, then it is easy for me to remember how many values I checked. But 1000 is a large number. So, to avoid checking more than 1000, I will also keep a count of how many values I checked until now. Whenever it reaches 1000, I will stop processing. This count also, I will write on a paper. 
3. Actually I don't have to iterate through whole array, once I got the lucky number. There is no point in counting frequency of any other number once we got the lucky number</attribute>
			<section name="graphics">
				<attribute key="x" type="double">479.66091498774637</attribute>
				<attribute key="y" type="double">3988.9494468858466</attribute>
				<attribute key="w" type="double">348.4091545813991</attribute>
				<attribute key="h" type="double">254.482998015921</attribute>
				<attribute key="type" type="String">rectangle</attribute>
				<attribute key="raisedBorder" type="boolean">false</attribute>
				<attribute key="fill" type="String">#FFCC00</attribute>
				<attribute key="outline" type="String">#000000</attribute>
			</section>
			<section name="LabelGraphics">
				<attribute key="text" type="String">Following are some hints to put the logic in words.

1. As I process 1000 values, it will be difficult for me to remember the frequency number in my memory. So, I will take a paper and write down the number with its frequency. That way, for every value, I will update the paper and I don't need to worry about forgetting.
2. If it is a small list like 3 or 5 values, then it is easy for me to remember how many values I checked. But 1000 is a large number. So, to avoid checking more than 1000, I will also keep a count of how many values I checked until now. Whenever it reaches 1000, I will stop processing. This count also, I will write on a paper. 
3. Actually I don't have to iterate through whole array, once I got the lucky number. There is no point in counting frequency of any other number once we got the lucky number</attribute>
				<attribute key="fontSize" type="int">12</attribute>
				<attribute key="fontName" type="String">Dialog</attribute>
				<attribute key="configuration" type="String">CroppingLabel</attribute>
				<attribute key="contentWidth" type="double">348.4091545813991</attribute>
				<attribute key="contentHeight" type="double">247.5</attribute>
				<attribute key="autoSizePolicy" type="String">node_width</attribute>
				<attribute key="anchor" type="String">c</attribute>
			</section>
		</section>
		<section name="node">
			<attribute key="id" type="int">44</attribute>
			<attribute key="label" type="String">Phase 1
1. Read the size of sequence from input
2. Create an empty array
3. Read the next value from input
4. Append it to the array
5. Repeat steps 3 and 4 until you read all values of the sequence
Phase 2
6. Create a new variable called count and initialize it to 1
7. Start from the second element of the array (Why second? Why not first?)
8. Take the element (why are we starting from second element and not first element?)
9. Compare it with the previous element
10. If current element and previous element have same value then increase the count by 1
11 Otherwise
11.1 Compare count value with the previous element value
11.2 If they are equal then you have found a lucky number. Stop and break out of the loop.
11.3 Otherwise reset the value of count to 1 (Why 1? Why not 0?)
12. Repeat steps 8 to 11 until we reach end of array.
13. We need to check if we came out of the loop becasue we found lucky number or because we reached end of the array.
14. If we found lucky number, then display it.
15. If we reached end of the array, then check if the frequency of last element is same as its value.
15.1 If yes then last element is the lucky number. Display it.
15.2 Otherwise there is no lucky number. Display -1.</attribute>
			<section name="graphics">
				<attribute key="x" type="double">264.87731537742195</attribute>
				<attribute key="y" type="double">4347.188332832493</attribute>
				<attribute key="w" type="double">806.6292049111071</attribute>
				<attribute key="h" type="double">344.5924301273707</attribute>
				<attribute key="type" type="String">rectangle</attribute>
				<attribute key="raisedBorder" type="boolean">false</attribute>
				<attribute key="fill" type="String">#FFCC00</attribute>
				<attribute key="outline" type="String">#000000</attribute>
			</section>
			<section name="LabelGraphics">
				<attribute key="text" type="String">Phase 1
1. Read the size of sequence from input
2. Create an empty array
3. Read the next value from input
4. Append it to the array
5. Repeat steps 3 and 4 until you read all values of the sequence
Phase 2
6. Create a new variable called count and initialize it to 1
7. Start from the second element of the array (Why second? Why not first?)
8. Take the element (why are we starting from second element and not first element?)
9. Compare it with the previous element
10. If current element and previous element have same value then increase the count by 1
11 Otherwise
11.1 Compare count value with the previous element value
11.2 If they are equal then you have found a lucky number. Stop and break out of the loop.
11.3 Otherwise reset the value of count to 1 (Why 1? Why not 0?)
12. Repeat steps 8 to 11 until we reach end of array.
13. We need to check if we came out of the loop becasue we found lucky number or because we reached end of the array.
14. If we found lucky number, then display it.
15. If we reached end of the array, then check if the frequency of last element is same as its value.
